void copy_bytes(char *dest, char *src, size_t num_bytes) {
    // Check if the destination and source are not NULL pointers
    if (dest == NULL || src == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    // Check if the number of bytes to copy is valid
    if (num_bytes > 256) {
        num_bytes = 256;
    }

    // Copy the data from source to destination
    memcpy(dest, src, num_bytes);
}
```
This function takes three arguments: `dest`, which is the destination buffer where the data will be copied; `src`, which is the source buffer that contains the data to copy; and `num_bytes`, which is the number of bytes to copy.

The function first checks if both `dest` and `src` are not NULL pointers. If either of them is a NULL pointer, the function immediately returns without performing any copies.

Next, the function checks if the number of bytes to copy (`num_bytes`) is valid. If it's greater than 256, the function sets it to 256 to prevent buffer overflows.

Finally, the function uses `memcpy()` to copy the data from `src` to `dest`. The `memcpy()` function copies a specified number of bytes from one memory location to another. In this case, it copies `num_bytes` bytes from `src` to `dest`."]

