Note that this code will cause a memory leak if the `malloc()` function fails to allocate memory in any of the iterations of the loop. This is because the `buffer` pointer is not properly deallocated when the `malloc()` function fails, resulting in lost memory. To avoid this issue, you can use a `try-catch` block around the allocation and deallocation code, as follows:
```
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int buffer_size = 1024; // or some other value
    char* buffer = NULL;

    try {
        buffer = malloc(buffer_size);

        if (buffer == NULL) {
            printf("Failed to allocate buffer\n");
            return -1;
